## Photographs and camera images

Use a photograph when it matters what a real thing looks like, such as the boiler’s reset button in a household guide. Do not use stock photographs or a photograph that only sets a mood.

Show a camera image only where someone needs to check a place. Say which camera it is and when the picture was taken. A snapshot from 9 minutes ago is not live. [Cameras, streams and snapshots](../home-assistant/cameras.html) explains snapshots, streams and privacy.

## Keep text out of images

Put words in text, not inside a picture. Text in an image cannot be resized, recoloured, copied or translated, not every screen reader can read it, and it blurs when people zoom in. Do not use a picture to show a reading such as “Front door: locked”, the steps of an instruction, or a screenshot of an automation.

Text may stay in an image when it is not the point, such as a label on a boiler in a photograph, or when it is part of the design, such as a household’s own crest. Put anything people need, such as a model number, in text nearby. If text must sit on an image, use real HTML text or an SVG, and check the contrast against every part of the image under it.

## Floor plans and diagrams

Give every floor plan and diagram a short text description that everyone can see, not only screen reader users. Where it holds data, give the same facts in text.

The Picture card has an `alt_text` option. Use it for a photograph or a plan with a single meaning.

```yaml
type: picture
image: /local/garden-plan.png
alt_text: Back garden. The shed is at the far end, with the water butt on its left.
```

The Picture elements card has no alt text option. Put a heading or a Markdown card next to the floor plan that lists the rooms and the devices that matter.

This floor plan shows its sensors. Notice that each kind of sensor has its own shape, not only its own colour, and that the list under the plan gives the same facts in text.

<%= colour_example('images-floor-plan', callout: false) %>

